      "problem": "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=(x-y)^{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{2}) \\\\  & =2(x-y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)^{2}) \\\\  & =-2(x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =2(x-y)\\,i-2(x-y)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,1),$ $(1,2),$ and $(1,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1) & =2\\cdot (2-1)\\,i-2\\cdot (2-1)\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,2) & =2\\cdot (1-2)\\,i-2\\cdot (1-2)\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =2\\cdot (1-(-1))\\,i-2\\cdot (1-(-1))\\,j=4\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14129144/assets/96851fd456d3b9f0a6af941a255926f35e29bbe778ed05556af3f69145e38ef2.png)",
